as none. J1 has not been melded by anyone. But J1 is a dragon, potentially useful for Two Dragon Pungs if I get J2 or J3 later \u2014 but I don\u2019t have other dragons.\n\nMy winning potential:  \nCurrently I have 2 melds, need 2 more + pair. If I keep T2,T3,T6, maybe I can get T4,T5 for chows. Keeping T8 pair as potential pair. B6 pair also potential pair.\n\nBest discard:  \nT8 \u2014 if I discard one T8, I break the pair but keep B6 pair as possible pair. But T8 is relatively safe (3 left) and not an honor. Alternatively discard J1 \u2014 J1 is honor and less useful unless I get two more, but J1 pung gives Dragon Pung (2 points). But I need two more J1 (only 2 left). Unlikely.\n\nI think T6 is least useful in terms of chow potential with current tiles (only 1 left in wall, and I have T2,T3 but not T4,T5,T7 in hand to combine with T6). Discarding T6 might be safe (only 1 left) and opens hand to focus on T2,T3,T8,B6,J1.\n\nBut B6 pair is good because B6 has many left, can possibly form pung or keep as pair.\n\nChoosing between T6, T2, T3, T8, J1:  \nT2 and T3 together can form chow if I draw T1 or T4, but T1 is out (0 left), T4 has 2 left, so maybe keep both.  \nJ1 is less useful unless I aim for Dragon Pung, but that\u2019s only 2 points.  \nT8 is a pair, if I keep I have a ready pair; if I discard one T8 I lose that pair option but can use B6 as pair.\n\nBetter to keep B6 pair and T8 pair? But I have only one pair in winning hand. Two pairs redundant unless going for Seven Pairs, which requires 7 pairs \u2014 impossible with 6 tiles left.\n\nActually, with 6 tiles in hand and 2 melds already, to complete normal hand: I need 4 tiles to form two melds and keep a pair. Keeping B6 pair and T8 pair means I have two pairs \u2014 I can break one later when I complete a meld. So keep both pairs for now.\n\nSo discard a non-pair tile: T2, T3, T6, or J1.  \nJ1 is riskier to discard because someone might need Dragon for pattern. But none have shown J1 meld. Previous player played J1 once (history: \u201cPlay J1\u201d) so maybe not collecting J1. Across played Peng W4, Peng F4 \u2014 maybe collecting winds.  \n\nSafest discard: T3 (4 left) or T2 (3 left) or T6 (1 left).  \nBut T6 has only 1 left, so discarding it might be safe because it\u2019s scarce.  \nBut also T6 could be useful if I draw T5 and T7 to make chow T5,T6,T7. Unlikely with only 1 T6 left.\n\nBetter discard: T3 \u2014 keep T2,T6 possibility for chows with T4,T5.  \nBut T3 can combine with T1,T2 or T4,T5? Chow needs consecutive, so T3 needs T1,T2 or T2,T4 or T4,T5 \u2014 not directly unless with T1,T2 (T1 gone) or T2,T4 (T4 available 2 left). Actually T2,T3,T4 is possible if I get T4. So keep T2,T3 together.\n\nTherefore discard T6 \u2014 least chow potential with current tiles, only 1 left.\n\nReason:  \nT6 is isolated, with only 1 copy left in the wall, making it both safe to discard and less useful for forming chows compared to keeping T2 and T3 together for a possible T2,T3,T4 chow if I draw T4. Keeping both B6 pair and T8 pair maintains flexibility for a pair. Discarding T6 reduces hand clutter without sacrificing much potential.\n\nAnswer: Play T6"}
